        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Who is Tonco Modderman's spouse?\n\nAnswer A: Anthonia Forsten\n  about: Tonco Modderman's spouse\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Tonco Modderman and Anthonia Forsten: A Marriage of Note\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: John Shawcross\n  about: Tonco Modderman's spouse\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Tonco Modderman strolled hand in hand with his beloved husband, John Shawcross.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Tonco Modderman and Anthonia Forsten: A Marriage of Note\n\nTonco Modderman (1745-1802) was a Dutch lawyer, poet, writer, and politician, while Anthonia Forsten (1753-1806) was a woman of prominence in her own right. According to historical records and credible sources, Tonco Modderman and Anthonia Forsten were married, a union that spanned several decades. Early Life and Career of Tonco Modderman\n\nTonco Modderman was born in 1745 in the Netherlands. He pursued a career in law, becoming a respected lawyer in his community. Modderman's literary talents also earned him recognition, as he was a prolific poet and writer. His writings often explored themes of love, nature, and social commentary. In addition to his literary pursuits, Modderman was involved in politics, serving in various capacities throughout his life. Anthonia Forsten: A Life of Her Own\n\nAnthonia Forsten was born on June 22, 1753, in Wildervank, Netherlands. Little is known about her early life, but it is clear that she came from a respectable family. Forsten's life took a significant turn when she married Tonco Modderman, a union that would last for many years. The Marriage of Tonco Modderman and Anthonia Forsten\n\nAccording to historical records, Tonco Modderman and Anthonia Forsten were married. This union is supported by multiple sources, including:\n\n* The Netherlands' national archives, which contain records of the marriage between Modderman and Forsten. [1]\n* Biographical accounts of Tonco Modderman, which consistently mention his marriage to Anthonia Forsten. [2]\n* Genealogical records, which confirm the marriage and provide details about their family life. [3]\n\nThe marriage between Tonco Modderman and Anthonia Forsten was likely a significant event in their lives, given their respective social standings and accomplishments. While little is known about the specifics of their relationship, it is clear that they were devoted to each other and shared a life together. Legacy of Tonco Modderman and Anthonia Forsten\n\nTonco Modderman's literary and political contributions continue to be celebrated in the Netherlands. His poetry and writings remain a testament to his talent and dedication to his craft.\nDocument 2: As the sun dipped into the Amsterdam canals, casting a warm orange glow over the city, Tonco Modderman strolled hand in hand with his beloved husband, John Shawcross. The year was 1780, and the air was alive with the whispers of revolution and reform. Tonco, a renowned lawyer and poet, had just finished a rousing speech at the local tavern, advocating for the rights of the common man. John, a quiet but fierce supporter, had been by his side every step of the way. As they walked, the cobblestone streets beneath their feet seemed to hum with the rhythm of Tonco's latest poem, \"The People's Cry.\" John, with his piercing green eyes and silver-streaked hair, smiled knowingly as Tonco recited the verses, his voice rising and falling like the city's famous church bells. Their stroll took them past the bustling market square, where vendors hawked their wares and the smell of fresh bread wafted through the air. Tonco, ever the romantic, paused to purchase a bouquet of wildflowers from a young girl with a mischievous grin. He presented them to John with a gallant bow, earning a soft blush and a gentle kiss on the cheek. As they continued their leisurely pace, they chanced upon a group of rowdy students, gathered around a makeshift stage in the city square. A young man, no more than twenty, stood atop a crate, passionately reciting Tonco's own words back to the crowd. The poet's eyes sparkled with pride as John leaned in, his voice low and husky, \"You've inspired a new generation, my love.\" Tonco's heart swelled with emotion, knowing that his words had found a home in the hearts of the people. He squeezed John's hand, feeling the familiar comfort of his husband's presence. It was a moment like this, surrounded by the beauty and chaos of Amsterdam, that Tonco knew he owed it all to John – his partner, his confidant, and his soulmate.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
